{"id":"mixup-augmentation","t":"MixUp of a batch","tags":["Computer Vision","Training"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"mixup","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"**MixUp** trains on convex combinations of pairs of examples and of their labels. Given a mixing weight $\\lambda \\in [0, 1]$ and a permutation $\\pi$ of the batch:\n\n$$\\tilde x_i = \\lambda\\, x_i + (1 - \\lambda)\\, x_{\\pi(i)}, \\qquad \\tilde y_i = \\lambda\\, \\mathbf e_{y_i} + (1 - \\lambda)\\, \\mathbf e_{y_{\\pi(i)}}$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf e_k$ is the one-hot vector of class $k$. In practice $\\lambda \\sim \\mathrm{Beta}(a, a)$ and $\\pi$ is a random shuffle; here both are passed in so the result is deterministic.\n\nWrite `mixup(x, labels, num_classes, lam, perm)` and return `(mixed_x, mixed_y)`.\n\n## Input\n- `x`: array `(B, ...)` of any numeric dtype (images `(B, H, W, C)`, features `(B, D)`, ...).\n- `labels`: int array `(B,)` in `0..num_classes-1`.\n- `lam`: float in `[0, 1]`. `perm`: int array `(B,)`, a permutation of `0..B-1`.\n\n## Output\n- `mixed_x`: `float64` array with the shape of `x`.\n- `mixed_y`: `float64` array `(B, num_classes)`. Each row sums to 1. When $y_i = y_{\\pi(i)}$ the row is simply one-hot.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef mixup(x, labels, num_classes, lam, perm):\n    \"\"\"Return (mixed_x float64 like x, mixed_y float64 (B, num_classes)).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Indexing the batch with `perm` gives the partner of every example at once: `x[perm]`.","One-hot labels in one line: `np.eye(num_classes)[labels]`.","Apply the same weighted sum to inputs and to one-hot labels, after casting `x` to float."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef mixup(x, labels, num_classes, lam, perm):\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\n    onehot = np.eye(num_classes)[np.asarray(labels)]          # (B, K)\n    mixed_x = lam * x + (1 - lam) * x[perm]\n    mixed_y = lam * onehot + (1 - lam) * onehot[perm]\n    return mixed_x, mixed_y\n","ex_md":"## Why mixing helps\nMixUp asks the model to behave **linearly** between training examples: halfway between a cat and a dog image, predict half cat and half dog. That regularises the decision boundaries, improves calibration, and reduces memorisation of noisy labels. With $\\mathrm{Beta}(a, a)$ and small $a$ (0.2-0.4), most $\\lambda$ values are close to 0 or 1, so most mixed images still look like one of the two.\n\n## Training with soft targets\nThe loss is the cross-entropy against the soft label, which equals $\\lambda\\,\\mathrm{CE}(\\hat y, y_i) + (1 - \\lambda)\\,\\mathrm{CE}(\\hat y, y_{\\pi(i)})$. Many implementations compute it that way and never build $\\tilde y$.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(B \\cdot \\text{example size})$, one extra copy of the batch.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Mixing in `uint8`: `0.3 * x` on a uint8 array is fine, but `x + x[perm]` before scaling overflows.\n- Using a different permutation for inputs and labels.\n- Mixing a batch with itself in the same order (`perm = arange(B)`), which does nothing.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How does MixUp interact with label smoothing? (Both soften targets; stacking them double-counts.)\n- What's \"manifold mixup\"? (The same interpolation applied to hidden activations at a random layer.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[ 0., 10.],\n       [20., 30.]])\narray([0, 1])\n2\n0.75\narray([1, 0])","out":"(array([[ 5., 15.],\n       [15., 25.]]), array([[0.75, 0.25],\n       [0.25, 0.75]]))"},{"in":"array([[1.],\n       [2.],\n       [3.]])\narray([2, 2, 0])\n3\n0.5\narray([1, 2, 0])","out":"(array([[1.5],\n       [2.5],\n       [2. ]]), array([[0. , 0. , 1. ],\n       [0.5, 0. , 0.5],\n       [0.5, 0. , 0.5]]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/deep-learning/training-deep-networks/dropout-and-regularisation/","t":"Dropout and regularisation","c":"Deep Learning"}],"related":["cutmix-augmentation","cross-entropy-logits"],"ntests":6,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 10.0], [20.0, 30.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0, 1], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, 2, 0.75, {\"__nd__\": [1, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[5.0, 15.0], [15.0, 25.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.75, 0.25], [0.25, 0.75]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0], [2.0], [3.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2, 2, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, 3, 0.5, {\"__nd__\": [1, 2, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.5], [2.5], [2.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0, 0.0, 1.0], [0.5, 0.0, 0.5], [0.5, 0.0, 0.5]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[[252, 118, 203], [224, 147, 172], [5, 41, 93]], [[70, 243, 219], [216, 161, 125], [146, 122, 35]], [[97, 108, 166], [244, 139, 96], [67, 66, 116]]], [[[72, 68, 225], [171, 82, 96], [205, 92, 167]], [[130, 237, 197], [175, 221, 15], [6, 91, 211]], [[50, 228, 248], [216, 190, 114], [202, 223, 47]]], [[[130, 247, 91], [64, 47, 180], [161, 54, 218]], [[171, 252, 105], [54, 143, 69], [137, 129, 212]], [[132, 103, 89], [237, 212, 172], [175, 117, 72]]], [[[55, 151, 154], [183, 254, 43], [81, 52, 152]], [[18, 221, 8], [247, 228, 138], [143, 210, 254]], [[137, 113, 198], [233, 153, 53], [62, 69, 74]]]], \"dtype\": \"uint8\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0, 1, 2, 3], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}, 5, 0.3, {\"__nd__\": [3, 2, 1, 0], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[[114.1, 141.1, 168.7], [195.3, 221.89999999999998, 81.7], [58.199999999999996, 48.699999999999996, 134.29999999999998]], [[33.6, 227.59999999999997, 71.3], [237.7, 207.89999999999998, 134.1], [143.89999999999998, 183.6, 188.29999999999998]], [[124.99999999999999, 111.5, 188.39999999999998], [236.3, 148.79999999999998, 65.89999999999999], [63.5, 68.1, 86.6]]], [[[112.6, 193.29999999999998, 131.2], [96.1, 57.5, 154.79999999999998], [174.2, 65.39999999999999, 202.7]], [[158.7, 247.49999999999997, 132.6], [90.3, 166.39999999999998, 52.8], [97.69999999999999, 117.6, 211.7]], [[107.3999999999999